\b 6. Discussion and Comparison\b0\line
The isentropic case provides the baseline acceleration and expansion behavior through the nozzle. Introducing friction modifies momentum balance and generally reduces the idealized acceleration trend, changing the Mach distribution along the axis. Heat addition modifies total temperature and therefore changes the local speed of sound and static-temperature trajectory, which in turn alters Mach and pressure development. When both effects are present, the profiles depart the most from the isentropic baseline due to simultaneous viscous and thermal source terms.\line
\line
The pressure plot is shown on a logarithmic scale (semilogy) as required so that differences among cases remain visible across the full magnitude range. Use of a small dx and adaptive halving near sonic conditions removes numerical glitches and prevents non-physical jumps close to the throat.\line
\line
\b 7. Conclusion\b0\line
A MATLAB model based on Shapiro Chapter 8 was implemented to compute and compare M(x), T(x), and p(x) for four nozzle-flow scenarios. The submitted figures satisfy assignment requirements by using x measured from throat (x = 0), semilog pressure plotting, and smooth numerical resolution near M = 1.\line
